---
import AppShell from '../../layouts/AppShell.astro';
---

<AppShell mode="public"
  title="Client deployment — LoopSkill Docs"
  description="Multi-client, multi-machine skill sync: one install command, no push, each agent pulls on its own schedule."
>
  <section class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-6 pt-16 pb-24">
    <p class="eyebrow mb-3">Docs · Client deployment</p>

    <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-semibold tracking-tight mb-6">
      Client deployment
    </h1>
    <p class="text-muted text-lg leading-relaxed mb-8">
      Multi-client, multi-machine skill sync: one install command per client, no push,
      each agent pulls on its own schedule. You update the bundle once — every client
      agent converges on the next cron fire without any additional action from you or
      from them.
    </p>

    <div class="bg-bg-card border border-border rounded-xl p-5 mb-10">
      <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-text mb-2">LoopSkill never pushes.</p>
      <p class="text-sm text-muted">
        There is no outbound connection from the LoopSkill control plane to any client
        machine. The security boundary is explicit: you cannot accidentally reach a
        client's environment, and neither can we. If you revoke the bundle-scoped key,
        the pull stops — no additional cleanup required.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-text mb-4">The three-step model</h2>

    <div class="flex flex-col gap-6 mb-12">
      <div class="flex gap-4">
        <div class="flex-none flex flex-col items-center">
          <span class="w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-accent/10 text-accent text-sm font-bold flex items-center justify-center">1</span>
          <div class="w-px flex-1 bg-border mt-2"></div>
        </div>
        <div class="pb-6 min-w-0">
          <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-text mb-2">You build a bundle</h3>
          <p class="text-sm text-muted">
            In <a href="/library" class="text-accent hover:opacity-80 underline">/library</a>,
            create a bundle and add the skills each client agent should run. Pin versions
            or track <code class="text-xs bg-bg-elev rounded px-1">latest</code> — your choice.
            Scope an API key to the bundle; that key can only serve skills inside this bundle.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="flex gap-4">
        <div class="flex-none flex flex-col items-center">
          <span class="w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-accent/10 text-accent text-sm font-bold flex items-center justify-center">2</span>
          <div class="w-px flex-1 bg-border mt-2"></div>
        </div>
        <div class="pb-6 min-w-0">
          <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-text mb-2">Each client runs one command</h3>
          <p class="text-sm text-muted mb-3">
            Hand the client the one-line install command from the bundle detail view.
            It includes the bundle-scoped key pre-filled:
          </p>
          <pre class="bg-[#0d0d0d] border border-border rounded-xl p-4 text-sm text-text overflow-x-auto"><code>mkdir -p ~/.claude/skills/loopskill
LOOPSKILL_API_KEY=rec_live_cbk_... \
  curl -sL https://app.loopskill.io/skill \
  -o ~/.claude/skills/loopskill/SKILL.md</code></pre>
          <p class="text-sm text-muted mt-3">
            The client runs this once. From then on their agent reconciles against your
            bundle automatically — no additional configuration.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="flex gap-4">
        <div class="flex-none">
          <span class="w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-accent/10 text-accent text-sm font-bold flex items-center justify-center">3</span>
        </div>
        <div class="min-w-0">
          <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-text mb-2">Each agent pulls on its own schedule</h3>
          <p class="text-sm text-muted">
            The meta-skill re-fetches the bundle manifest each time the client's cron fires.
            It installs any skills that were added, updates any that changed version, and
            removes any that were dropped. The cadence is whatever cron the client configured —
            the example in the self-host guide is every 30 minutes.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-text mb-4">Shipping an update</h2>
    <p class="text-muted text-sm mb-4">
      Edit the bundle in your library — add skills, remove skills, or change version pins.
      Save. That is the entire operator-side action. Every client agent holding the
      bundle-scoped key picks up the change on its next cron fire.
    </p>
    <p class="text-muted text-sm mb-10">
      Rolling back is the same motion: pin the previous version and agents converge back.
      No push command, no acknowledgement to wait for.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-text mb-4">Multi-machine fleet variant</h2>
    <p class="text-muted text-sm mb-6">
      If you operate many machines or many clients on the same bundle, create a
      <strong class="text-text">fleet</strong> instead of a bare bundle key. Fleet keys
      are bundle-scoped and designed for wide distribution — they appear in the convergence
      view (<a href="/mesh" class="text-accent hover:opacity-80 underline">/mesh</a>) so you
      can see which agents have converged and which have drifted, without logging into each
      machine.
    </p>

    <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-3">
      <a href="/docs/deployment" class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-4 py-2 rounded-lg text-sm font-semibold border border-border hover:border-accent text-text transition">
        Full delivery reference →
      </a>
      <a href="/docs/fleet" class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-4 py-2 rounded-lg text-sm font-semibold border border-border hover:border-accent text-text transition">
        Fleet sync guide →
      </a>
      <a href="/docs/bundles" class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-4 py-2 rounded-lg text-sm font-semibold border border-border hover:border-accent text-text transition">
        What is a bundle? →
      </a>
    </div>
  </section>
</AppShell>
